WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.740
Obviously, anyone still breathing in the final
season of Game of Thrones is going to have

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:09.200
a pretty huge impact on the story, but one
man, Sandor Clegane, might end up being a

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.640
little more than just another tough guy with
a sword.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.640
This is why the Hound might be more important
than you realized.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.420
One of the reasons the Hound has become such
a fan favorite over the years is that he's

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:24.780
eminently relatable — in a world of dragons,
ice zombies, and Machiavellian sociopaths,

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.680
the only thing he really believes in is himself
and his sword.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:31.630
But as the show has ramped up its fantasy
tropes from the relatively grounded early

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:35.300
episodes, the Hound's continuing atheism has
become more and more of an important plot

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:35.800
point.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:41.040
After his near-death experience at the hands
of Brienne in season four, the Hound recuperated

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.940
under the watchful eye of Brother Ray.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:47.700
"It’s never too late to stop robbing people
to stop people and stop killing people and

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:48.780
start helping people.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:51.780
It's never too late to come back."

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.820
Shortly after, Ray is brutally killed by roving
members of the Brotherhood of Banners... because

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.580
it is Game of Thrones, after all.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:02.670
Still, the Hound's brief connection with the
man seems to have been setting up an about-face

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:03.820
for the consistently amoral swordsman.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.680
After all, it's only after Ray's death that
the Hound gets involved with the unkillable

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.460
zealot Beric Dondarrion, which leads him into
the path of the Night King and his unstoppable

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:13.240
zombie army.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.500
With Ray's religious teachings fresh in his
mind, the show has primed us for a powerful

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:21.680
moment when the Hound realizes there are things
bigger than him in the world — and no, we're

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.120
not just talking about this guy.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:28.320
Between the books, the television show, and
the many, many fan theories swirling around

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:32.610
the plot and its characters, you'd be forgiven
for losing track of some of the various prophecies

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.840
floating about in the Game of Thrones universe.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.610
Still, there's at least one that you should
definitely be thinking about before the final

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:39.610
season hits.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:43.800
That would be the prophecy that concerns Azor
Ahai, or the Prince That Was Promised.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.460
The basic gist is that Azor Ahai was a legendary
swordsman who was able to forge a sword so

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.240
powerful that it would routinely burst into
flames.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.900
This hero eventually fought against, and defeated,
a terrible darkness that threatened the entire

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:56.400
world.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:01.020
Just about every character on the show has
been theorized to be Azor Ahai reborn, although

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.200
Jon Snow and Daenerys are two of the fan favorites
by a mile.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.740
However, it also could be possible that the
Hound is Azor Ahai.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.489
The main crux of the theory is that it would
be poetic for the Hound — a man who believes

00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:15.890
in nothing and has a paralyzing fear of fire
— to wield a holy flaming blade against

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:18.140
the Night King in a grand show of selflessness.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:22.820
While it might be a bit of a stretch to suggest
that the Hound is reborn nobility, his relationship

00:02:22.829 --> 00:02:27.609
to the current wielder of a flaming sword,
Beric, definitely gives this theory some credence.

00:02:27.609 --> 00:02:31.689
The two have had a long history, and if Beric
were to actually die for good, it would only

00:02:31.689 --> 00:02:34.640
be fitting for the Hound to pick up his flaming
sword in battle.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.300
Okay, so there’s a good chance that the
Hound isn’t actually the fabled Prince That

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.540
Was Promised; in fact, that would be a pretty
late and unexpected twist for a show that's

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:47.049
generally been adept at setting up its plot
points before they unfold.

00:02:47.049 --> 00:02:50.799
Still, it's almost certain that the Hound
will resolve his phobia of fire, and there's

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.480
good reason to believe he'll do so by picking
up Beric's flaming sword.

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:58.820
As fans of Game of Thrones will know, the
Hound has a deathly fear of fire that began

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.160
after a childhood encounter with his older
brother.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:07.340
“It’s just like you said, a while back,
he pressed me to the fire like I was a nice,

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:08.600
juicy mutton chop.”

00:03:10.029 --> 00:03:13.999
It's character building 101 for a character
to overcome trauma over the course of their

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.780
arc, and the Hound's long overdue for some
flame-based exposure therapy.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:21.120
And it can only help that there’s already
a god in the world of Game of Thrones with

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.740
something of a penchant for fire… who has
already been in contact with the Hound.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:33.440
“The dead are marching past… thousands
of them.”

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.540
A number of other characters in the show have
expressed their faith in the Lord of Light,

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:41.420
but the Hound is the first non-follower we've
seen receive a vision from the god.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:45.360
It all adds up to the near certainty that
the Lord of Light is maneuvering a man terrified

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.580
of fire into doing his divine bidding.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:48.600
How poetic.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.680
If there's one single thing that Game of Thrones
fans truly want from the show’s final season,

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.370
it has to be Cleganebowl.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:59.989
A fan term for the long-awaited battle between
the Hound and his brother, Gregor Clegane,

00:03:59.989 --> 00:04:04.520
Cleganebowl has been demanded, doubted, and
in season seven, seemingly confirmed.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.880
“That’s not how it ends for you brother.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.620
You know who’s coming for you, you’ve
always known.”

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:17.470
The two brothers might have had a short-lived
duel back in season one, but they've both

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:19.250
been through the wringer since then.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:23.740
The Mountain was poisoned by Oberyn Martell,
which led to him becoming a mute, zombie-esque

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:25.380
slave to Cersei Lannister.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.940
Meanwhile, the Hound's been practically all
the way up and down Westeros in search of

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:29.640
purpose.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:33.880
But even after everything they’ve been through,
Sandor and Gregor don't seem to have forgotten

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:35.860
their childhood enmity for each other.

